WEBVTT

00:00.150 --> 00:02.760
- [Instructor] We're told
the polynomial p of x,

00:02.760 --> 00:06.840
which is equal to this, has
a known factor of x plus six.

00:06.840 --> 00:10.470
Rewrite p of x as a
product of linear factors.

00:10.470 --> 00:13.290
Pause this video, and see if
you can have a go at that.

00:13.290 --> 00:16.040
All right, now let's
work on this together.

00:16.040 --> 00:18.030
Because they give us one of the factors,

00:18.030 --> 00:19.340
what we can do is say hey,

00:19.340 --> 00:22.970
what happens if I divide
x plus six into p of x?

00:22.970 --> 00:24.200
What do I have left over?

00:24.200 --> 00:26.520
It looks like I'm still
going to have a quadratic,

00:26.520 --> 00:28.590
and then I'll probably
have to factor that somehow

00:28.590 --> 00:30.440
to get a product of linear factors.

00:30.440 --> 00:32.320
So let's get going.

00:32.320 --> 00:33.960
So if I were to try to figure out

00:33.960 --> 00:38.370
what x plus six divided into,

00:38.370 --> 00:42.240
x to the third plus nine x squared,

00:42.240 --> 00:43.710
and now we're gonna have to be careful.

00:43.710 --> 00:46.860
You might be tempted to
just write minus 108 there,

00:46.860 --> 00:48.250
but then this gets tricky

00:48.250 --> 00:49.930
because you have your third degree column,

00:49.930 --> 00:51.030
your second degree column.

00:51.030 --> 00:52.420
You need your first degree column,

00:52.420 --> 00:53.800
but you just put your zero degree,

00:53.800 --> 00:55.500
your constant column here.

00:55.500 --> 00:57.570
So to make sure we have good hygiene,

00:57.570 --> 01:00.200
we could write plus zero x,

01:00.200 --> 01:02.600
and I encourage you to
actually always do this

01:02.600 --> 01:04.550
if you're writing out a polynomial

01:04.550 --> 01:08.260
so that you don't skip
that place, so to speak,

01:08.260 --> 01:11.130
minus 108.

01:11.130 --> 01:13.280
And so then you say, all right,

01:13.280 --> 01:15.510
let's look at the highest degree terms.

01:15.510 --> 01:19.970
X goes into x to the
third x squared times.

01:19.970 --> 01:22.900
X squared times six is six x squared.

01:22.900 --> 01:25.180
X squared times x is x to the third.

01:25.180 --> 01:26.570
We want to subtract.

01:26.570 --> 01:27.770
We've done this multiple times,

01:27.770 --> 01:30.000
so I'm going a little
bit faster than normal.

01:30.000 --> 01:30.960
Those cancel out.

01:30.960 --> 01:35.020
Nine x squared minus six x
squared is three x squared.

01:35.020 --> 01:37.183
Bring down that zero x.

01:38.470 --> 01:42.130
And then how many times does
x go into three x squared?

01:42.130 --> 01:43.810
Well, it goes three x times,

01:43.810 --> 01:45.700
and we would write it in this column.

01:45.700 --> 01:48.210
And notice, if we didn't keep this column

01:48.210 --> 01:50.390
for our first degree terms,
we'd be kind of confused

01:50.390 --> 01:52.540
where to write that
three x right about now.

01:52.540 --> 01:57.450
And so three x plus, times
six, I should say, is 18 x.

01:57.450 --> 01:59.930
Three x times x is three x squared.

01:59.930 --> 02:01.750
We want to subtract what we have in that,

02:01.750 --> 02:05.120
I guess that color is mauve,
light purple, not sure.

02:05.120 --> 02:09.000
And so we get three x squareds cancel out,

02:09.000 --> 02:12.140
and then zero x minus 18 x

02:12.140 --> 02:14.420
is negative 18 x.

02:14.420 --> 02:18.500
Bring down that negative 108.

02:18.500 --> 02:21.180
And so then we have

02:21.180 --> 02:24.250
x goes into negative 18 x

02:24.250 --> 02:26.870
negative 18 times.

02:26.870 --> 02:30.690
Negative 18 times six is

02:30.690 --> 02:32.750
negative 108.

02:32.750 --> 02:34.300
That's working out nicely.

02:34.300 --> 02:37.430
Negative 18 times x is negative 18 x.

02:37.430 --> 02:38.820
And then we want to subtract

02:38.820 --> 02:41.120
what we have in this
not-so-pleasant brown color,

02:41.120 --> 02:44.280
and so I will multiply
them both by a negative.

02:44.280 --> 02:46.930
And so I am left with zero.

02:46.930 --> 02:48.790
Everything just cancels out.

02:48.790 --> 02:52.000
And so I can rewrite p of x.

02:52.000 --> 02:54.470
I can rewrite p of x

02:54.470 --> 02:57.900
as being equal to x plus six

02:57.900 --> 03:02.170
times x squared plus three x minus 18.

03:02.170 --> 03:04.990
But I'm not done yet because
this is not a linear factor.

03:04.990 --> 03:06.570
This is still quadratic.

03:06.570 --> 03:08.160
So let's see, can I think of two numbers

03:08.160 --> 03:10.000
that add up to three

03:10.000 --> 03:12.290
and that when I multiply
I get negative 18?

03:12.290 --> 03:14.000
So I'll need different signs,

03:14.000 --> 03:16.390
and then the obvious one

03:18.003 --> 03:21.950
is positive six and negative three.

03:21.950 --> 03:24.800
And if that, what I just did,
seems like voodoo to you,

03:24.800 --> 03:28.170
I encourage you to review
factoring polynomials.

03:28.170 --> 03:32.540
But this I can rewrite
'cause negative six plus,

03:32.540 --> 03:35.030
or actually I should say positive six

03:35.030 --> 03:37.490
plus negative three is equal to three,

03:37.490 --> 03:39.380
and then positive six times negative three

03:39.380 --> 03:40.900
is equal to negative 18.

03:40.900 --> 03:45.020
So I can rewrite this as x plus six times

03:45.020 --> 03:48.620
x plus six times

03:48.620 --> 03:51.420
x minus three.

03:51.420 --> 03:52.780
And so there we have it.

03:52.780 --> 03:55.770
We have a product of linear factors.

03:55.770 --> 03:56.883
And we are done.
